%S A390535 2,2,2,6,2,2,7,3,2,2,7,2,2,3,9,2,3,2,2,23,2,3,30,2,2,34,2,2,38,3,2,42,
%T A390535 3,2,23,3,2,2,2,3,2,3,41,2,3,2,31,2,2,2,9,2,3,7,2,2,74,2,3,2,39,3,2,
%U A390535 41,3,2,2,86,2,3,2,3,71,2,3,2,2,2,102,2,3,106
%N A390535 Smallest integer > 1 whose radix-A013929(n) congruence speed never stabilizes.
%C A390535 For a fixed integer n > 1, the radix-n congruence speed of every integer m > 1 not a multiple of n stabilizes (to a positive integer constant) if and only if n is squarefree (see A373387 for the radix-10 definition and A390598 for the constant congruence speed in radix-6).
%C A390535 Furthermore, for every integer n >= 1, a(n) exists (and, generally, 2 <= a(n) <= A013929(n)/2 holds) since a(n) cannot exceed the product of the distinct prime factors of A013929(n) (which, by definition, only consists of nonsquarefree terms). Indeed, in the radix-A013929(n) numeral system, the tetration a(n)^^k, for k = 2,3,4,... will eventually be congruent to 0 modulo A013929(n), and hence the radix-A013929(n) congruence speed of a(n) will rapidly accelerate for each unit increment of k.
%C A390535 If we restrict attention to integers a(n) that are coprime to A013929(n), no explicit upper bounds are currently known for the terms of the analogous sequence "Smallest integer > 1 and coprime to A013929(n) whose radix-A013929(n) congruence speed never stabilizes".
%C A390535 Exact criterion: for a prime p dividing A013929(n) but not m, let s_p = v_p(m^ord_p(m) - 1) if p is odd and s_2 = v_2(m^2 - 1) - 1. By the lifting-the-exponent lemma, v_p(m^^(k+1) - m^^k) = s_p*k + O(1) for these p, while it grows like a tower for p | m. Hence the radix-A013929(n) congruence speed of m never stabilizes if and only if m is divisible by every prime factor of A013929(n), or the minimum of s_p/v_p(A013929(n)) over the primes p | A013929(n) not dividing m is not an integer. - _Alper Ferudun_, Oct 20 2026
%D A390535 Marco Ripà, La strana coda della serie n^n^...^n, Trento, UNI Service, Nov 2011. ISBN 978-88-6178-789-6.
%H A390535 Gabriele Di Pietro, <a href="/A390535/b390535.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..194</a> (corrected by Alper Ferudun)
%H A390535 Marco Ripà, <a href="https://doi.org/10.7546/nntdm.2021.27.4.43-61">The congruence speed formula</a>, Notes on Number Theory and Discrete Mathematics, 2021, 27(4), 43—61.
%H A390535 Marco Ripà and Gabriele Di Pietro, <a href="https://doi.org/10.7546/nntdm.2026.32.3.477-496">A compact notation for peculiar properties characterizing integer tetration</a>, Notes on Number Theory and Discrete Mathematics, 2026, 32(3), 477-496 (see Appendix B).
%H A390535 Marco Ripà and Luca Onnis, <a href="https://doi.org/10.7546/nntdm.2022.28.3.441-457">Number of stable digits of any integer tetration</a>, Notes on Number Theory and Discrete Mathematics, 2022, 28(3), 441—457.
%H A390535 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetration">Tetration</a>.
%F A390535 For any given n > 1, let A013929(n) = p_1^q_1*p_2^q_2*...*p_k^q_k be the prime factorization of A013929(n), where p_1, p_2, ..., p_k are distinct primes. Then, a(n) <= p_1*p_2*...*p_k.
%e A390535 a(4) = 6 since the congruence speed of 6 does not converge to a fixed value in the radix-12 numeral system (it equals 0 at height 1, 3 at height 2, 23325 at height 3, and so forth).
%o A390535 (PARI) isns(m, r) = my(f=factor(r), t=oo); for(i=1, #f~, my(p=f[i, 1]); if(m%p, t=min(t, if(p==2, valuation(m^2-1, 2)-1, valuation(m^znorder(Mod(m, p))-1, p))/f[i, 2]))); t==oo || denominator(t)>1; \\ m never stabilizes in radix r
%o A390535 a(n) = my(r=1, c=0); while(c<n, r++; c+=!issquarefree(r)); for(m=2, oo, if(m%r && isns(m, r), return(m))); \\ _Alper Ferudun_, Oct 20 2026
%Y A390535 Cf. A013929, A317905, A373387 (radix-10 constant congruence speed), A379906, A380031, A390597, A390598 (radix-6 constant congruence speed), A390576.
